The field of forensic food analysis has a well-defined history of detecting these substances, though the exact techniques change as technology evolves. Common indicators include:

• **Hair analysis:** roots and foliage are analyzed for chemical residues;
• **Microscopy:** magnification reveals particles like cellulose, starch, or pigment contamination;
• **Polymer analysis:** using UV light to identify microbial degradation products;
• **Toxicology:** in some jurisdictions, pesticide residues can be identified through specialized laboratory testing.

Ethics in this work is critical because improper handling can endanger consumers. Modern protocols emphasize chain-of-custody documentation, proper disposal of samples, and avoiding contamination of control groups. If you're studying this, look into the NSDL (National Society of Crime Lab Directors) guidelines or forensic chemistry textbooks that cover detection methodologies.
